---
/* The index: what the series is, the one axis everything turns on, and the
 * essays by part, in reading order - nothing else. One column at the measure
 * of an essay, so the index and an essay share one left edge and one width. */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { PARTS } from "../lib/parts";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const essays = (await getCollection("essays")).sort((a, b) => a.data.number - b.data.number);
const href = (e: (typeof essays)[number]) => `${base}${e.id}/`;
const count = essays.length;
const description = `The ideas behind abap2UI5 in ${count} short technical essays: what it rests on, how it works under the app, a working day with it, where it belongs in the landscape and what running it takes.`;
/* the axis: three ways to put a UI5 screen in front of a user, by the moment
 * the view is fixed - HTML rather than a picture, so it reflows on a phone
 * and follows the reader's colour scheme */
const AXIS = [
  { name: "UI5 Freestyle", when: "build time", what: "a file in a frontend project" },
  { name: "RAP / Fiori Elements", when: "activation time", what: "UI annotations on CDS" },
  { name: "abap2UI5", when: "request time", what: "a string from an ABAP class", hit: true },
];
---
<Page title={`abap2UI5 Insights — ${count} essays on one idea`} description={description} trail={[{ text: "Insights" }]}>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Insights</h1>
    <p class="lead">What happens when the ABAP server sends the <em>screen</em> instead of only the data.</p>
    <p>
      {count} short essays, each making a single claim and showing it in code wherever code can show it. Read one on its
      own and it stands. Read them in order and the parts add up to the whole picture — what the
      framework rests on, how it works under the app, what a working day with it looks like,
      where it belongs in the landscape and what running it takes. They are the “why it works like
      this” for a developer and an architect.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is a good chance you will also pick up a few things about ABAP along the way. RTTS, the
      dialog step, <code>ENQUEUE</code>, the selection screen, the ICF node, the launchpad: this
      framework did not invent much, it just moved a lot of familiar furniture into a browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="axis">
    <div class="axis-card">
      <p class="axis-head">When the view is fixed</p>
      <ol class="axis-cols">
        {AXIS.map((a) => (
          <li class={a.hit ? "hit" : undefined}>
            <span class="axis-name">{a.name}</span>
            <span class="axis-when">{a.when}</span>
            <span class="axis-what">{a.what}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
    <figcaption>One axis: when the definition of the screen stops being changeable.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="after-axis">
    Three ways to put a UI5 screen in front of a user on an ABAP stack, all three rendering in the
    browser with the same controls. They differ in one thing: when the view is fixed. In abap2UI5 it
    is a string an ABAP class produces for this request — and the next request may answer
    differently. Everything else follows from that.
  </p>

  <section class="parts" aria-label="The essays, in reading order">
    {PARTS.map((part, i) => (
      <section class="part" id={part.id}>
        <header>
          <span class="part-no">Part {i + 1}</span>
          <h2>{part.title}</h2>
          <p class="lead">{part.lead}</p>
        </header>
        <ol class="essays">
          {essays.filter((e) => e.data.part === part.id).map((e) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href(e)}>
                <span class="n">#{e.data.number}</span>
                <span class="t">{e.data.title}</span>
                <span class="d">{e.data.blurb}</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    ))}
  </section>
</Page>
